WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:05.970
The rate of scientific discovery is getting
faster and faster, with major discoveries

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.600
happening every year, rather than every century.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:14.000
Mars landings, gravitational waves, cancer
treatments, vegan chili con carne, we really

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.439
do seem to be able to achieve the unthinkable.

00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:20.119
But that’s not to say we do it all on our
own.

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:24.609
Since the dawn of civilization, animals have
been more than just our faithful companions

00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:28.900
and our delicious lunch; they have been a
vital part of our understanding of how the

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:34.060
world works and often an inspiration for how
to make it a better place.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:40.540
So go give Barkin’ Scorsese an extra cookie,
let Meowly Cyrus up on the bed, and together

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:49.330
you can learn about 7 animals who have helped
advance science.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.650
Dogs need space, lots of it.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:56.920
Although the Soviets may have taken this a
bit far with the legendary dog Laika, when

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:02.480
they fired her out the atmosphere and made
her the first animal to orbit the earth.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:09.390
Mankind has had a long history of firing animals
into the sky, since 1738 when the Montgolfier

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:13.230
brothers sent a sheep, a duck and a rooster
up in a hot air balloon.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:17.360
The sheep was named Montauciel, which means
climb-to-the-sky.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.920
They believed a sheep to have roughly similar
physiology to a human and the duck and rooster

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.680
were control animals, this was science after
all.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.530
But also because they thought it was funny
to see how a sheep, a duck and rooster get

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.870
on together in a spaceship.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:35.510
This set the standard and by the 1940s, the
skies were filled with balloons and rockets

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:40.220
being tested by frogs, fruit flies, fish and
monkeys, among many others.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.030
It was like a Disney movie directed by Michael
Bay.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:49.750
Laika was sent into orbit on November 3, 1957,
to test how both the g-force of the launch

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.740
and micro-gravity would affect a living organism.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:57.369
Poor Laika just believed that someone had
thrown a stick really, really far.

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:03.119
The brave beast died within hours from overheating
but there was never any intention of bringing

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:07.659
her home, which many of the scientists say
they regretted later on.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:13.850
If you were ever mugged by a sheep and had
to pick one out of a police line-up, you’d

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.110
probably struggle.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:21.230
This may be why the Roslin Institute in the
University of Edinburgh, decided to use sheep

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:26.230
as clone subjects; if it didn’t work, they
could have just grabbed any other sheep and

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.440
said “look, see, they’re just like twins.”

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:34.540
But work it did and Dolly the Sheep was born
on 5 July 1996.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.599
She was named Dolly since the original cell
was taken from a mammary gland, which made

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:44.130
the team think of Dolly Parton, the famously
glandular singer.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:49.209
Like an extreme episode of the Jeremy Kyle
show, Dolly had 3 different mothers; one gave

00:02:49.209 --> 00:02:53.540
the egg, one gave the DNA and the final one
did the whole pregnancy thing.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.170
Actually I think I watched that episode last
week.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:02.060
Although not the first cloned animal, Dolly
was the first clone of an adult, proving that

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.930
you could revert an organism back to the embryonic
state.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:10.850
The process is called somatic cell nuclear
transfer, where you take out the nucleus of

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:16.340
a developing egg cell and put in the nucleus
of a developed adult cell; like putting your

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:21.060
grandad in a pair of Converse and skinny jeans
and seeing if he fits in at Skrillex concert.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.060
More Red Bull grandpops?

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:27.709
Thanks to Dolly, we may be able to preserve
endangered species and even revive extinct

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:30.830
ones by the process she helped to pioneer.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:37.000
Do you find your goldfish suspicious, always
staring at you with those goggly eyes and

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.620
gormless expression?

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.560
Well, it may be a soviet spy.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:47.070
We’ve used many different beasts for spying
over the centuries; pigeons carrying messages,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:51.220
dogs that can sniff out weapons and cats that
are really good at hacking; well I assume

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:53.820
that’s why they always sit on your keyboard
looking suspicious.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:04:00.630
Recently, Hamas captured an aquatic spy near
the coast of the Gaza Strip, it was a dolphin.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:05.540
Both Israeli and Palestinian media said that
Double O Dolphin was equipped with recording

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.879
devices and even an arrow-firing weapon.

00:04:08.879 --> 00:04:13.640
It’s thought that he was there to take video
of the frogmen training facility, presumably

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:18.739
so they could plan an attack using a ninja
octopus and a really angry seahorse.

00:04:18.739 --> 00:04:22.790
Dolphin secret agents are not a new thing
though; we’ve been using them since the

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:27.660
1960s for a variety of porpoises (purposes).

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:34.370
Both the US and the Soviets had dolphin teams
to spot underwater mines and they still do

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:39.910
since their echo location is still far superior
to any tech we’ve been able to create so

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:40.910
far.

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:46.600
As far as killing goes, there have been no
reported incidents but ex-Navy Seal, Brandon

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:51.690
Webb, talked in his memoir about training
dolphins to head-butt enemy targets with a

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:56.470
compressed needle, which could inject lethal
bubbles of CO2 into their blood.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:59.130
Beware the killer spy dolphins.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:03.620
The Israelis have been accused of having a
whole zoo of animal agents, from masterminding

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:09.190
the shark attack in Sharm el Sheikh to a surveillance
vulture captured in Saudi Arabia.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:13.639
I’m looking forward to Bacon Bourne, the
swine sleeper agent.

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:19.250
Speaking of porky animals, Ham the Chimp was
another great in the story of space exploration.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:24.600
Ham was born in what was then The French Cameroons
but was shipped to the US at a young age to

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:30.910
join the School for Space Chimps at the Holloman
Air Force Base in Alamagordo, New Mexico.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:36.200
This was all part of Project Mercury, the
NASA mission to put a human into space.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:41.669
On the last day of January 1961, Ham was strapped
into a nappy and then into a rocket where

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:47.300
he spent 16 minutes being successfully launched
up to zero gravity and then coming back down

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:48.860
in his capsule.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:53.540
He helped NASA gain valuable data about the
life support systems they would use soon after

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:58.510
for Alan Shepard, the first American and second
human to enter space.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:01.900
But how did Ham feel about all this?

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.880
Famous primate expert Jane Goodall was highly
critical of the mission and on reviewing the

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.840
footage said that she had never seen a chimp
look more terrified.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:16.600
Sergio Canavero is a maverick surgeon who
has been working his way up the animal chain

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.830
with the dubious aim of performing a full
human head transplant.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:27.160
Dr. Vladimir Demikhov led a lot of our advancement
in the field of transplanting, performing

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:33.210
a lot of different operations in the 40s and
50s and pioneering the use of immuno-suppressants

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.820
so that the body would accept the new body
part.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:41.300
And in 1959 he even transplanted a dog’s
head onto a new body.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:46.860
Neurosurgeon, Robert J. White, performed a
successful monkey head transplant in 1970

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:51.150
with the animal surviving long enough to see,
smell and hear the world around it.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.699
It then decided it didn’t think much of
the world around it and proceeded to bite

00:06:54.699 --> 00:06:55.860
people.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:01.229
So it’s undoubtedly possible for humans
but the ethical questions it raises are pretty

00:07:01.229 --> 00:07:04.190
big; how do you define a person?

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:06.139
Am I only me above the nipples?

00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:07.139
Above the neck?

00:07:07.139 --> 00:07:11.880
And what if the body I’m transplanted onto
has a One Direction tattoo; death would probably

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.520
be a better option.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:20.300
Our final scientific assistant is a wee bionic
beast who, like his namesake Oscar Pistorius,

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.050
bounces around on blades.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:26.830
Oscar lost both his back legs in a nasty accident
with a combine harvester, down on the channel

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:28.419
island of Jersey.

00:07:28.419 --> 00:07:32.949
His owners rejected advice to have him put
down and soon they were communicating with

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:39.009
neuro-orthopaedic surgeon Noel Fitzpatrick
in the UK and organizing for Oscar to have

00:07:39.009 --> 00:07:45.080
specially engineered, honeycomb-shaped implants
that would be drilled directly into his remaining

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:46.520
ankle bones.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:51.419
This technique was designed to mimic how deer
antlers grow and allow skin to grow over the

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:55.729
point where the implant joins the body and
so prevent infection.

00:07:55.729 --> 00:08:00.569
The whole process probably cost about £50,000
but most of that was covered by the pioneering

00:08:00.569 --> 00:08:05.069
doctors, rather than the owners and the knowledge
they gained has now been used to help humans,

00:08:05.069 --> 00:08:09.630
such as a woman injured in the July 2005 London
bombings.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:14.020
So rather than just putting your hamster on
a wheel or getting your puppy to play fetch,

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:18.949
maybe try something a little more challenging
as your pet may have the key to the cure for

00:08:18.949 --> 00:08:45.690
cancer or they could just teach you a really
useful shortcut on Microsoft excel.

